# 1. How does HashMap work internally?

> **"HashMap is a key-value data structure used to store data for fast retrieval. Internally, it uses an array of buckets. When we insert a key-value pair, Java calculates the key's `hashCode()` and determines the bucket index. If the bucket is empty, it stores the entry directly. If multiple keys map to the same bucket, it handles collisions using a LinkedList, and in Java 8 and above, it converts the list into a Red-Black Tree when the bucket size becomes large for better performance. During retrieval, it again calculates the hash code and uses `equals()` to find the correct key. The average time complexity for put and get operations is O(1). In my project, I used HashMap to cache configuration data and lookup values because it provides very fast access in single-threaded scenarios."**

---

# 2. What is the difference between HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap?

> **"The main difference is that HashMap is not thread-safe, whereas ConcurrentHashMap is thread-safe and designed for multi-threaded applications. HashMap allows one null key and multiple null values, while ConcurrentHashMap doesn't allow null keys or values. Internally, ConcurrentHashMap uses fine-grained locking and CAS (Compare-And-Swap) operations, allowing multiple threads to read and write simultaneously without locking the entire map. This provides better concurrency than Hashtable. In one of my projects, multiple scheduled jobs updated an in-memory cache while REST APIs were reading the same data. We used ConcurrentHashMap to avoid race conditions and ensure thread safety while maintaining good performance."**

