# 7. How do you handle exceptions in enterprise applications?

> **"In enterprise applications, I follow centralized exception handling using Spring Boot's @RestControllerAdvice and @ExceptionHandler annotations. Instead of handling exceptions in every controller, we create custom exception classes for business scenarios and manage them from a single global exception handler. We also log exceptions using SLF4J and return meaningful HTTP status codes such as 400 for validation errors, 404 for resource not found, and 500 for unexpected server errors. In my project, this approach ensured consistent error responses across all REST APIs, simplified maintenance, and made debugging much easier."**

---

# 8. Explain synchronization. Have you faced thread-safety issues?

> **"Synchronization is a mechanism in Java that allows only one thread to access a critical section of code at a time, preventing race conditions and maintaining data consistency. It can be implemented using synchronized methods, synchronized blocks, or Lock implementations. Without synchronization, multiple threads may update shared data simultaneously, leading to inconsistent results. In one of my projects, multiple scheduled jobs updated an in-memory cache while REST APIs were reading it concurrently. Initially, we observed inconsistent data due to concurrent updates. We replaced HashMap with ConcurrentHashMap, which resolved the thread-safety issue by allowing safe concurrent reads and writes while maintaining good performance."**

